from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('15');add=A.add
add('3.3.1-01','Conduction transfers thermal energy through matter without bulk movement of that matter.','Particles and, in metals, mobile electrons transfer energy through interactions.','A metal spoon can warm along its length while the spoon stays in place. The atoms need not travel from the hot end to the cold end. Energy transfer is driven by a temperature difference. A material with low thermal conductivity reduces this transfer but does not stop every transfer mechanism.','Define thermal conduction.','It is thermal energy transfer through matter without bulk movement of the material.')
add('3.3.1-02','A fair conduction comparison uses rods with matched dimensions and heating conditions.','A faster temperature rise at the same distance can indicate more rapid conduction.','Heat one end of equal-length and equal-thickness rods of different materials using a common controlled source. Place temperature sensors at equal distances and record their changes over equal times. A wax indicator can show arrival of heating, but sensor readings give a more direct comparison. Keep the unheated surfaces exposed similarly and avoid touching hot ends.','Describe a fair test of two materials’ thermal conduction.','Heat matched rods equally and compare temperature rises at equal distances after the same time.')
add('3.3.1-03','In nonmetals, neighbouring particles transfer thermal energy through interactions.','Metals also have mobile electrons that transfer energy through the material.','A hotter region has more energetic microscopic motion. Interactions pass energy toward cooler regions without the whole solid flowing. Mobile electrons contribute strongly to metals’ conductivity. Trapped air is useful insulation when its bulk movement is restricted, because it conducts poorly and cannot easily form large convection currents.','Explain why a metal can conduct thermal energy better than a comparable nonmetal.','Both transfer energy through particle interactions.~The metal additionally has mobile electrons that carry energy through it.')
add('3.3.2-01','Natural convection is energy transfer by bulk fluid movement caused by density differences in a gravitational field.','The moving liquid or gas carries internal energy.','Convection requires a fluid able to move. A heated region commonly expands and becomes less dense; surrounding denser fluid can displace it upward. Cooling regions sink and complete circulation where the arrangement permits it. A solid block cannot transfer energy by an internal convection current because its material does not flow in this way.','Define natural convection.','It is thermal energy transfer by bulk fluid motion driven by density differences in a gravitational field.')
add('3.3.2-02','Conduction occurs without bulk material flow, whereas convection carries material through the system.','Both can operate in different regions of one heating arrangement.','At a hot metal-water boundary, conduction transfers energy into nearby water. The warmed water can then circulate by convection through the vessel. Naming the whole process simply heat rising misses the cause: fluid rises under buoyancy because of density differences, while thermal energy can also transfer downward by conduction.','Explain how conduction differs from convection.','Conduction transfers energy without bulk movement of material.~Convection carries energy with the bulk motion of a liquid or gas.')
add('3.3.2-03','Heating can make a fluid region expand and become less dense than its surroundings.','Buoyancy can then drive it upward while denser fluid moves into its place.','State expansion and density before the direction of flow. Warm fluid does not rise merely because its particles have an upward preference. If heating occurs from above, the warmer low-density region may remain at the top, reducing natural circulation. The arrangement and ability to move therefore matter.','Explain a convection current produced by heating water from below.','The lower water warms, expands and becomes less dense.~It rises while cooler denser water moves down to replace it, setting up circulation.')
add('3.3.2-04','A tracer can reveal bulk fluid circulation in a convection demonstration.','Local heating should be arranged so the tracer shows the moving fluid rather than stirring by hand.','Place a small suitable dye tracer near the bottom of a transparent water vessel and heat gently beneath that side. Observe coloured water rising near the heated region and a return path elsewhere. Do not stir during the observation. Use a safe heat source and heat-resistant vessel; strong boiling would obscure the intended gentle circulation.','Describe how to demonstrate convection in water.','Use a dye tracer and gentle local heating from below to reveal rising heated water and a cooler return flow.')
add('3.3.3-01','Thermal radiation transfers energy by electromagnetic waves.','It does not require a material medium.','Energy from the Sun reaches Earth across space by radiation. A vacuum prevents ordinary conduction and convection across the gap, but does not prevent radiation. A body can emit and absorb radiation simultaneously; its net energy change depends on both processes and other transfers.','Define thermal radiation.','It is thermal energy transfer by electromagnetic waves without requiring a material medium.')
add('3.3.3-02','Infrared radiation is part of the electromagnetic spectrum.','It is associated strongly with thermal emission from bodies at ordinary temperatures.','Infrared is not a moving hot gas and is not visible red light. It travels across a vacuum like other electromagnetic radiation. The detailed spectrum is taught in topic 19; here its role is energy transfer, absorption and emission.','State the type of wave that infrared radiation is.','It is an electromagnetic wave.')
add('3.3.3-03','Surface finish affects infrared absorption and emission.','Comparisons need controlled area, temperature and measurement geometry.','A matt dark surface is commonly a good absorber and emitter compared with a shiny metallic surface in the school model. An emission test starts with surfaces at the same temperature; an absorption test exposes matched surfaces to equal incident radiation. Cooling rates alone combine several transfer mechanisms, so use controlled conditions before attributing a difference solely to radiation.','Explain why an infrared emission comparison and an absorption comparison need different controls.','Link emission to equal surface temperature and detector geometry.~For absorption, equal incident radiation and matched thermal capacities are needed to compare resulting warming.')
add('3.3.3-03-M01','An emission comparison measures radiation from differently finished surfaces at the same temperature.','Equal detector distance and exposed area help make it fair.','A vessel with matt-black and shiny-metal faces can hold water at one common temperature. Measure infrared detector response facing each surface at the same distance and angle. A larger response indicates greater emission under those conditions. Avoid changing temperature during the sequence or warming the detector by contact.','Describe a fair infrared-emission comparison of black and shiny surfaces.','Keep their temperatures equal and compare a detector’s response at equal distance and angle.')
add('3.3.3-03-M02','An absorption comparison exposes matched samples to equal incident radiation.','A greater temperature rise can indicate greater absorption if other energy effects are controlled.','Use equal cans with different surface finishes, equal water masses and equal starting temperatures. Place them equal distances from the same source with equal exposed areas. Record temperature rises over the same time. Match draughts and insulation so conduction and convection differences do not dominate the inference.','Describe a fair infrared-absorption test of two surface finishes.','Expose matched samples with equal starting temperatures to equal radiation and compare their temperature rises over the same time.')
add('3.3.4-01','Insulating designs often address conduction, convection and radiation together.','A single surface treatment seldom removes every transfer pathway.','A vacuum flask uses low-conductivity supports and a stopper, a vacuum gap and reflective surfaces. The gap reduces conduction and convection; the shiny surfaces reduce radiation. A lid also limits warm-air movement and evaporation from the contents. Explain each feature by the pathway it affects rather than saying a flask stops heat completely.','Explain why a flask can need both a vacuum gap and reflective surfaces.','Combine mechanisms: the vacuum reduces conduction and convection across the gap.~Reflective surfaces reduce radiation, which can still cross a vacuum.')
add('3.3.4-01-M01','Low-conductivity handles reduce conduction from a hot vessel to the hand.','A metal base can instead promote useful conduction into food or water.','The choice depends on the intended energy pathway. A good conductor is useful where rapid transfer is wanted, while an insulator is useful at a contact needing protection. A handle can still become hot by radiation or direct flame, so the mechanism explanation is conditional on its arrangement.','Explain why a saucepan can have a metal base and an insulating handle.','The base conducts energy efficiently into the contents.~The handle reduces conduction toward the hand.')
add('3.3.4-01-M02','Natural convection can carry warm air upward from a room heater.','Placement and air circulation affect how the room warms.','Air near the heater expands and becomes less dense, rises, and is replaced by cooler air. An opening high in a warm enclosure can permit warm air to escape; a lower opening can admit cooler replacement air. Blocking circulation can reduce this effect.','Explain why a room heater near the floor can set up circulation.','Nearby air warms, expands and becomes less dense.~It rises and cooler air flows in below, carrying energy around the room.')
add('3.3.4-01-M03','Shiny metallic surfaces can reduce infrared exchange compared with matt-black surfaces under comparable conditions.','A dark absorber is useful where collecting incident radiation is the purpose.','A solar collector benefits from absorption, while a flask surface benefits from reduced radiative transfer. These choices have opposite purposes. Do not infer infrared behaviour from visible colour alone for every advanced material; the ordinary school comparison concerns the named surface finishes.','Explain why a solar absorber and a heat-retaining flask may use different finishes.','The collector benefits from strong radiation absorption.~The flask benefits from reducing radiative transfer with suitable shiny surfaces.')
A.misconception('MIS-11','Conduction across a heater-fluid boundary and convection through the fluid describe different stages.','Bulk water circulation is not conduction simply because a solid heater starts the transfer.','In a heated pot, energy first reaches adjacent water through the material boundary. Warmer water can then move upward and carry energy elsewhere in the fluid. A complete explanation can use both mechanisms without assigning the same name to every region.','A metal heater warms water that then circulates upward. Explain why calling the entire transfer conduction is incomplete.','The correct boundary process can involve conduction without bulk material flow.~The moving water carries energy by convection; the confusion is between boundary transfer and fluid transport.')
C=A.choice
C('3.3.1-01','Which process needs no bulk flow of material?',('Conduction','Energy passes through interactions.'),('Natural convection','The fluid moves.'),('A warm-air current','This involves moving material.'),('A circulating water stream','This transports matter.'))
C('3.3.1-03','What extra carriers help thermal conduction in metals?',('Mobile electrons','They transfer energy through the metal.'),('Macroscopic boiling bubbles','These are not the solid-metal mechanism.'),('A wind inside the solid','The solid does not convect.'),('Created atoms','Energy transfer does not create matter.'))
C('3.3.2-01','Which material can sustain ordinary internal convection?',('A liquid','Its bulk material can move.'),('A rigid solid block','It cannot flow internally this way.'),('A vacuum','It contains no fluid to circulate.'),('An isolated empty region','No medium is present.'))
C('3.3.3-01','Which transfer can cross a vacuum?',('Radiation','Electromagnetic waves need no matter.'),('Ordinary conduction','It needs material interactions across the path.'),('Convection','It needs moving fluid.'),('Water circulation','Water must be present.'))
C('3.3.3-02','Infrared belongs to which group?',('Electromagnetic waves','It shares their vacuum propagation.'),('Sound waves','Sound needs a medium.'),('Liquid currents','These are material flow.'),('Electron streams by definition','Infrared is radiation, not a charged-particle stream.'))
C('3.3.3-03-M01','What must match in an emission comparison?',('Surface temperature','Emission depends strongly on temperature.'),('Different detector distances','These would confound the comparison.'),('Different exposed areas','These can change signal.'),('One surface being heated much more','This confounds finish with temperature.'))
C('3.3.3-03-M02','Which finish is a good absorber in the named school comparison?',('Matt black','It absorbs strongly compared with shiny metal.'),('Polished metal','It reflects much incident radiation.'),('No surface at all','There is nothing to test.'),('A finish selected without specifying properties','That cannot establish absorption.'))
C('3.3.2-03','Water is heated gently from below. Why can the warmed region rise?',('It becomes less dense and is displaced upward','Expansion changes density.'),('Its particles become heavier','Heating does not add mass.'),('Heat itself has an upward-only direction','Energy can transfer in other directions.'),('Water becomes a solid immediately','That is not the stated effect.'))
C('3.3.4-01','Why are shiny walls useful across a flask’s vacuum gap?',('They reduce radiation','Radiation can cross the gap.'),('They stop a convection current in the vacuum','No fluid is there to circulate.'),('They add water to the vacuum','That would reduce insulation.'),('They create heat','No energy is created.'))
C('3.3.1-02','Two rods have different lengths and thicknesses. What is the issue in comparing conduction?',('Geometry confounds material effects','Matched dimensions are needed.'),('It becomes an exact material-only test','Geometry influences the result.'),('Units are unnecessary','Measurements still need units.'),('They cannot have temperatures','Both can have temperature.'))
C('3.3.4-01-M01','Why use an insulating handle on a hot pot?',('To reduce conduction to the hand','Its low conductivity limits that pathway.'),('To improve heat flow into the hand','That is the opposite purpose.'),('To make the pot weightless','Mass and weight are not removed.'),('To prevent all radiation nearby','The handle does not remove radiation.'))
C('3.3.4-01','Water cools from 70 to 64 °C in 3 min. What is its average temperature gradient?',('−2 °C/min','The signed change is −6 °C over 3 min.'),('2 °C/min rising','The temperature falls.'),('−6 °C/min','This ignores division by time.'),('−18 °C/min','This multiplies by duration.'))
P=A.part
P('3.3.3-01','Name the transfer mechanism that crosses a vacuum.','Radiation.',1,'One mark for radiation.')
P('3.3.1-03','Name the mobile charge carriers that aid conduction in metals.','Electrons.',1,'One mark for electrons.')
P('3.3.2-03','Explain upward flow near the bottom heater in water.','The heated water expands and becomes less dense. Denser surrounding water displaces it upward.',2,'One mark for density decrease; one for buoyancy-driven movement.')
P('3.3.4-01','Explain why a lid can slow a hot drink’s cooling.','It reduces removal of warm air and limits evaporation from the liquid surface.',2,'One mark for reduced convection/air movement; one for reduced evaporation.')
P('3.3.4-01','Calculate the average temperature-time gradient when a sample falls from 68 to 60 °C in 4 min.','Gradient = (60 − 68)/4 = −2 °C/min.',2,'One mark for signed change divided by time; one for −2 °C/min.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot temperature vertically against time horizontally and fit the short approximately linear falling section.',unit='°C/min',meaning='The negative gradient is temperature decrease per minute. Its magnitude is an average cooling rate for the stated short interval, not a universal cooling constant.',method=['Put the same measured mass of warm water in a covered container.','Record the starting temperature and ambient temperature.','Start a timer and record the stirred water temperature at regular short intervals.','Repeat with a different insulation layer using a fresh run at the same initial temperature.','Compare corresponding short intervals and repeat each arrangement.'],control='Keep water mass, container, lid, starting temperature and room conditions matched; avoid draughts and handle warm water safely.',table='Record time / min and water temperature / °C for each insulation arrangement, with ambient temperature noted.',conclusion='Compare the magnitudes of gradients at similar water-to-room temperature differences; a smaller magnitude indicates slower cooling under those conditions.')
if __name__=='__main__':A.build()
